361†Installation L417:         * cite362†Executing SQL L418:         * cite363†Jupyter Notebooks L419:         * cite364†marimo Notebooks L420:         * cite365†SQL on Pandas L421:         * cite366†Import from Pandas L422:         * cite367†Export to Pandas L423:         * cite368†Import from Numpy L424:         * cite369†Export to Numpy L425:         * cite370†SQL on Arrow L426:         * cite371†Import from Arrow L427:         * cite372†Export to Arrow L428:         * cite373†Relational API on Pandas L429:         * cite374†Multiple Python Threads L430:         * cite375†Integration with Ibis L431:         * cite376†Integration with Polars L432:         * cite377†Integration with PyTorch L433:         * cite378†Using fsspec Filesystems L434:       * SQL Editors\nL435:         * cite379†DBeaver SQL IDE L436:       * SQL Features\nL437:         * cite380†AsOf Join L438:         * cite381†Full-Text Search L439:         * cite382†Graph Queries L440:         * cite383†query and query_table Functions L441:         * cite384†Merge Statement for SCD Type 2 L442:         * cite385†Timestamp Issues L443:       * Snippets\nL444:         * cite386†Creating Synthetic Data L445:         * cite387†Dutch Railway Datasets L446:         * cite388†Sharing Macros L447:         * cite389†Analyzing a Git Repository L448:         * cite390†Importing Duckbox Tables L449:         * cite391†Copying an In-Memory Database to a File L450:         * cite392†Calculating a Database Checksum L451:       * Troubleshooting\nL452:         * cite393†Command Line L453:         * cite394†Crashes L454:         * cite395†Out of Memory Errors L455:       * cite396†Glossary of Terms L456:       * cite397†Browsing Offline L457:     * Operations Manual\nL458:       * cite398†Overview L459:       * DuckDB's Footprint\nL460:         * cite399†Files Created by DuckDB L461:         * cite400†Gitignore for DuckDB L462:         * cite401†Reclaiming Space L463:       * Installing DuckDB\nL464:         * cite402†Install Script L465:       * Logging\nL466:         * cite403†Overview L467:       * cite404†User Agents L468:       * Securing DuckDB\nL469:         * cite405†Overview L470:         * cite406†Embedding DuckDB L471:         * cite407†Securing Extensions L472:       * cite408†Non-Deterministic Behavior L473:       * cite409†Limits L474:       * cite410†DuckDB Docker Container L475:     * Development\nL476:       * cite411†DuckDB Repositories L477:       * cite412†Release Cycle L478:       * cite413†Metrics L479:       * cite414†Profiling L480:       * Building DuckDB\nL481:         * cite415†Overview L482:         * cite416†Build Configuration L483:         * cite417†Building Extensions L484:         * cite418†Android L485:         * cite419†Linux L486:         * cite420†macOS L487:         * cite421†Raspberry Pi L488:         * cite422†Windows L489:         * cite423†Python L490:         * cite424†R L491:         * cite425†Troubleshooting L492:         * cite426†Unofficial and Unsupported Platforms L493:       * cite427†Benchmark Suite L494:       * Testing\nL495:         * cite428†Overview L496:         * cite429†sqllogictest Introduction L497:         * cite430†Writing Tests L498:         * cite431†Unit Tester Configuration L499:         * cite432†Debugging L500:         * cite433†Result Verification L501:         * cite434†Persistent Testing L502:         * cite435†Loops L503:         * cite436†Multiple Connections L504:         * cite437†Catch L505:     * Internals\nL506:       * cite438†Overview L507:       * cite439†Storage Versions and Format L508:       * cite440†Execution Format L509:       * cite441†Jemalloc L510:       * cite442†Pivot L511:   * cite443†Sitemap L512:   * cite444†Live Demo†shell.duckdb.org L513: Copy Markdown\nL514: \nL515: Reading and Writing Parquet Files\nL516: ## cite445†Examples L517: \nL518: Read a single Parquet file:\nL519: \nL520:     `SELECT * FROM 'test.parquet';\nL521:     `\nL522: \nL523: Figure out which columns/types are in a Parquet file:\nL524: \nL525:     `DESCRIBE SELECT * FROM 'test.parquet';\nL526:     `\nL527: \nL528: Create a table from a Parquet file:\nL529: \nL530:     `CREATE TABLE test AS\nL531:         SELECT * FROM 'test.parquet';\nL532:     `\nL533: \nL534: If the file does not end in `.parquet`, use the `read_parquet` function:\nL535: \nL536:     `SELECT *\nL537:     FROM read_parquet('test.parq');\nL538:     `\nL539: Use list parameter to read three Parquet files and treat them as a single table:\nL540: \nL541:     `SELECT *\nL542:     FROM read_parquet(['file1.parquet', 'file2.parquet', 'file3.parquet']);\nL543:     `\nL544: \nL545: Read Parquet files from a local directory, including its subdirectories:\nL546: \nL547:     `SELECT *\nL548:     FROM read_parquet('test');\nL549:     `\nL550: \nL551: A directory path recursively reads files with the `.parquet` extension. To read only files directly inside the directory, use `test/*.parquet` instead. See cite446†directory paths for details.\nL552: Read all files that match the glob pattern:\nL553: \nL554:     `SELECT *\nL555:     FROM 'test/*.parquet';\nL556:     `\nL557: \nL558: Read all files that match the glob pattern, and include the `filename` virtual column that specifies which file each row came from (this column is available by default without a configuration options since DuckDB v1.3.0):\nL559: \nL560:     `SELECT *, filename\nL561:     FROM read_parquet('test/*.parquet');\nL562:     `\nL563: Use a list of globs to read all Parquet files from two specific folders:\nL564: \nL565:     `SELECT *\nL566:     FROM read_parquet(['folder1/*.parquet', 'folder2/*.parquet']);\nL567:     `\nL568: \nL569: Read over HTTPS:\nL570: \nL571:     `SELECT *\nL572:     FROM read_parquet('https://some.url/some_file.parquet');\nL573:     `\nL574: \nL575: Query the cite447†metadata of a Parquet file :\nL576: \nL577:     `SELECT *\nL578:     FROM parquet_metadata('test.parquet');\nL579:     `\nL580: \nL581: Query the cite448†file metadata of a Parquet file :\nL582: \nL583:     `SELECT *\nL584:     FROM parquet_file_metadata('test.parquet');\nL585:     `\nL586: Query the cite449†key-value metadata of a Parquet file :\nL587: \nL588:     `SELECT *\nL589:     FROM parquet_kv_metadata('test.parquet');\nL590:     `\nL591: \nL592: Query the cite450†schema of a Parquet file :\nL593: \nL594:     `SELECT *\nL595:     FROM parquet_schema('test.parquet');\nL596:     `\nL597: \nL598: Write the results of a query to a Parquet file using the default compression (Snappy):\nL599: \nL600:     `COPY\nL601:         (SELECT * FROM tbl)\nL602:         TO 'result-snappy.parquet'\nL603:         (FORMAT parquet);\nL604:     `\nL605: Write the results from a query to a Parquet file with specific compression and row group size:\nL606: \nL607:     `COPY\nL608:         (FROM generate_series(100_000))\nL609:         TO 'test.parquet'\nL610:         (FORMAT parquet, COMPRESSION zstd, ROW_GROUP_SIZE 100_000);\nL611:     `\nL612: \nL613: Export the table contents of the entire database as parquet:\nL614: \nL615:     `EXPORT DATABASE 'target_directory' (FORMAT parquet);\nL616:     `\nL617: ## cite451†Parquet Files L618: \nL619: Parquet files are compressed columnar files that are efficient to load and process. DuckDB provides support for both reading and writing Parquet files in an efficient manner, as well as support for pushing filters and projections into the Parquet file scans.\nL620: > Parquet datasets differ based on the number of files, the size of individual files, the compression algorithm used, row group size, etc. These have a significant effect on performance. Please consult the cite452†Performance Guide for details.\nL621: ## cite453†`read_parquet` Function L622: \nL623: Function  | Description  | Example\nL624: --- | --- | ---\nL625: `read_parquet(path_or_list_of_paths)`  | Read Parquet file(s)  | `SELECT * FROM read_parquet('test.parquet');`\nL626: `parquet_scan(path_or_list_of_paths)`  | Alias for `read_parquet`  | `SELECT * FROM parquet_scan('test.parquet');`\nL627: \nL628: If your file ends in `.parquet`, the function syntax is optional. The system will automatically infer that you are reading a Parquet file:\nL629: \nL630:     `SELECT * FROM 'test.parquet';\nL631:     `\nL632: Multiple files can be read at once by providing a glob or a list of files. Refer to the cite454†multiple files section for more information.\nL633: ### cite455†Parameters L634: \nL635: There are a number of options exposed that can be passed to the `read_parquet` function or the cite456†`COPY` statement .\nL636: Name  | Description  | Type  | Default\nL637: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL638: `binary_as_string`  | Parquet files generated by legacy writers do not correctly set the `UTF8` flag for strings, causing string columns to be loaded as `BLOB` instead. Set this to true to load binary columns as strings.  | `BOOL`  | `false`\nL639: `can_have_nan`  | Whether `FLOAT` and `DOUBLE` columns may contain `NaN` values. When set to true, the reader accounts for `NaN` when using a column's min/max statistics for filter pushdown, since `NaN` does not compare as ordered.  | `BOOL`  | `false`\nL640: `encryption_config`  | Configuration for cite457†Parquet encryption .  | `STRUCT`  | -\nL641: `filename`  | Whether or not an extra `filename` column should be included in the result. Since DuckDB v1.3.0, the `filename` column is added automatically as a virtual column and this option is only kept for compatibility reasons.  | `BOOL`  | `false`\nL642: `file_row_number`  | Whether or not to include the `file_row_number` column.  | `BOOL`  | `false`\nL643: `hive_partitioning`  | Whether or not to interpret the path as a cite458†Hive partitioned path .  | `BOOL`  | (auto-detected)\nL644: `union_by_name`  | Whether the columns of multiple schemas should be cite459†unified by name , rather than by position.  | `BOOL`  | `false`\n"
